# Polar coordinates: coord_radial

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`coord_radial()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/coord_radial.html), which covers
`coord_polar()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and
then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting
for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

In polar coordinates one position becomes an angle around a centre and the other a distance from
it; a pie chart is a stacked bar chart drawn this way. `coord_polar()` is superseded by
`coord_radial()`, whose options choose which of `x` and `y` is the angle (`theta`), where the
circle starts and ends, the limits of the angle and the radius, whether the scales expand, the
size of a hole in the middle, and where the radius axis goes. `Giggly.Plot.coord_radial/1` is
ggplot2's `coord_radial()` without options, so it draws as `coord_radial()` does with every
option at its default. Giggly has no `coord_polar()` yet.

## A pie chart

```r
# NOTE: Use these plots with caution - polar coordinates has
# major perceptual problems.  The main point of these examples is
# to demonstrate how these common plots can be described in the
# grammar.  Use with EXTREME caution.

# A pie chart = stacked bar chart + polar coordinates
pie <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = factor(1), fill = factor(cyl))) +
 geom_bar(width = 1)
pie + coord_radial(theta = "y", expand = FALSE)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_bar()`'s `width` option and `coord_radial()`'s `theta` and `expand`
options.

## A coxcomb plot

```r
# A coxcomb plot = bar chart + polar coordinates
cxc <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_bar(width = 1, colour = "black")
cxc + coord_radial(expand = FALSE)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_bar()`'s `width` option and `coord_radial()`'s `expand` option.

## Counts as angles

```r
# A new type of plot?
cxc + coord_radial(theta = "y", expand = FALSE)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_bar()`'s `width` option and `coord_radial()`'s `theta` and `expand`
options.

## A bullseye chart

```r
# The bullseye chart
pie + coord_radial(expand = FALSE)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_bar()`'s `width` option and `coord_radial()`'s `expand` option.

## A pie chart's colours and starting angle

```r
# Hadley's favourite pie chart
df <- data.frame(
  variable = c("does not resemble", "resembles"),
  value = c(20, 80)
)
ggplot(df, aes(x = "", y = value, fill = variable)) +
  geom_col(width = 1) +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("red", "yellow")) +
  coord_radial("y", start = pi / 3, expand =  FALSE) +
  labs(title = "Pac man")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_col()`'s `width` option, `scale_fill_manual()`, and `coord_radial()`'s
`theta`, `start` and `expand` options.

## A wind rose and a race track

R's `if (require("ggplot2movies"))` runs the example only when the `ggplot2movies` package, which
holds the `movies` dataset, is installed.

```r
# Windrose + doughnut plot
if (require("ggplot2movies")) {
movies$rrating <- cut_interval(movies$rating, length = 1)
movies$budgetq <- cut_number(movies$budget, 4)

doh <- ggplot(movies, aes(x = rrating, fill = budgetq))

# Wind rose
doh + geom_bar(width = 1) + coord_radial(expand = FALSE)
# Race track plot
doh + geom_bar(width = 0.9, position = "fill") +
  coord_radial(theta = "y", expand = FALSE)
}
```

Not in Giggly yet: the `movies` dataset, `cut_interval()` and `cut_number()`, `geom_bar()`'s `width`
option, `position_fill()`, and `coord_radial()`'s `theta` and `expand` options.

## A partial circle

```r
# A partial polar plot
ggplot(mtcars, aes(disp, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  coord_radial(start = -0.4 * pi, end = 0.4 * pi, inner.radius = 0.3)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `coord_radial()`'s `start`, `end` and `inner.radius` options.

## Limits

```r
# Similar with coord_cartesian(), you can set limits.
ggplot(mtcars, aes(disp, mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  coord_radial(
    start = -0.4 * pi,
    end = 0.4 * pi, inner.radius = 0.3,
    thetalim = c(200, 300),
    rlim = c(15, 30),
  )
```

Not in Giggly yet: `coord_radial()`'s `start`, `end`, `inner.radius`, `thetalim` and `rlim`
options.
